WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
In this video, we'll work through the iteration loop that happens after Claude proposes a plan.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:14.000
You don't have to accept a plan as is. You can edit, reject, or trigger a replan mid-execution.

3
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:19.000
We'll cover the full loop from approval through replanning, look at pairing patterns,

4
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:24.000
and identify the failure modes that undermine plan mode in practice.

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.000
Every plan Claude proposes arrives as a draft, not a verdict.

6
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.000
The approval gate is the checkpoint where you decide what happens to it.

7
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:39.000
You can accept it as is, edit it inline or reject it entirely, and restate your intent.

8
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.000
And nothing is written to disk until you pass through.

9
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.000
The gate fails when you approve without reading.

10
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Skimming a plan and clicking through hands controls straight back to Claude.

11
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Treat it the way you treat a code review. Fast is fine. Absent isn't.

12
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.000
Iterative refinement is the broader pattern behind that gate.

13
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.000
You draft, review, and revise until the plan captures your intent.

14
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.000
Plan mode pairing extends it by combining plan mode with subagents that investigate first

15
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.000
or hooks that validate steps before execution.

16
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Editing a plan before approval is faster than triggering a full replan,

17
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.000
so it's worth understanding what you can change.

18
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.000
The most common edit is trimming scope.

19
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Claude sometimes interprets a request more broadly than you intended,

20
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.000
and a plan that proposes touching 8 files when you wanted 3 is a signal to narrow it before proceeding.

21
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Reordering steps is also common.

22
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:41.000
You might know that a dependency has to be updated before a migration can run,

23
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.000
even if Claude sequenced them differently.

24
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:49.000
And occasionally, you'll add a step Claude omitted, based on your knowledge of the codebase.

25
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.000
After you make edits, approve the revised plan.

26
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Claude executes your version, not the original.

27
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.000
What happens when execution starts and reality doesn't match the plan?

28
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.000
This is where replanning comes in.

29
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.000
The clearest trigger is when a file Claude expected to exist doesn't,

30
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.000
or when an assumption about the codebase turns out to be wrong.

31
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.000
The right response is to stop, surface the divergence explicitly,

32
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.000
and regenerate the plan before continuing.

33
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:22.000
The question is when replanning is actually necessary versus when it's overkill.

34
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:27.000
If the broken assumption only affects one step and the rest of the plan is still valid,

35
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.000
you might be able to patch and proceed.

36
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.000
If the broken assumption undermines several downstream steps, replanning is the right call.

37
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:42.000
The decision depends on how much of the remaining work depends on the assumption that broke.

38
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.000
Plan mode pairing adds reliability layers to the core pattern.

39
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.000
The first pattern is subagent investigation.

40
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:53.000
A subagent reads the codebase, gathers context, and returns findings.

41
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:56.000
The main agent builds the plan from those findings.

42
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:02.000
Investigation and planning stay separate, so the plan rests on richer information.

43
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.000
The second pattern is hook validation.

44
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.000
Pre-execution hooks verify plan steps before Claude runs them.

45
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.000
A hook might check that a file exists or that a linter passes.

46
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.000
The third pattern is human in the loop, or staged human review.

47
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:22.000
Instead of one approval gate at the start, you approve after each major phase.

48
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.000
Staged gates catch drift before it compounds.

49
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:28.000
The fourth pattern combines all three.

50
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.000
A subagent finds, a hook validates, and a human approves.

51
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:38.000
Each layer catches a different failure class, and stacking them yields the highest reliability.

52
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.000
The most common failure in plan mode workflows

53
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:45.000
is when Claude abandons the plan and improvises mid-execution.

54
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.000
It usually starts with an unexpected file state.

55
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.000
Claude encounters something the plan didn't account for

56
00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:55.000
and adapts silently rather than surfacing the divergence.

57
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:59.000
It adds steps that weren't in the plan, adjusts its approach,

58
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:02.000
and you end up reviewing code that doesn't match what you approved.

59
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.000
The fix is behavioral.

60
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:10.000
Before execution starts, tell Claude explicitly to stop and surface any divergence

61
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.000
rather than working around it.

62
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.000
Something like, if you encounter anything that contradicts the plan,

63
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.000
pause and tell me before proceeding.

64
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.000
That instruction doesn't guarantee Claude will always do it,

65
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:26.000
but it reduces the frequency of silent improvisation significantly.

66
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.000
A few workflow habits make plan mode consistently useful.

67
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.000
Keep plans short.

68
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:37.000
Five to eight discrete steps is the right range for a plan you can actually review.

69
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:41.000
Plans with 20 steps are hard to check and hard to edit.

70
00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:47.000
Name files explicitly rather than using vague references like the relevant config file.

71
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:51.000
If Claude can't name the file, the plan isn't complete enough to approve.

72
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.000
Always ask for risks.

73
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:56.000
Even when you think you know the code base well,

74
00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.000
asking Claude to flag assumptions surfaces things you might have overlooked.

75
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.000
These three habits, short plans, explicit file names, and explicit risk statements

76
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:10.000
produce plans that are reviewable, editable, and reliable.

77
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:15.000
It's worth stepping back and framing the tradeoff between plan mode and direct execution.

78
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.000
Plan mode has higher upfront overhead.

79
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:23.000
You wait for a proposal, read it, possibly edit it, and then approve.

80
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:28.000
That overhead is justified when the work is multi-file, ambiguous, or hard to reverse.

81
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:33.000
If the plan catches a major wrong assumption before any code is written,

82
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:37.000
the total time is lower than discovering the mistake after execution.

83
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:40.000
Direct execution has lower overhead.

84
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:46.000
Claude writes code immediately, which is faster for small, reversible, single-file changes

85
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:49.000
where the cost of a mistake is low.

86
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.000
The decision isn't plan mode, always versus never.

87
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:56.000
It's matching the tool to the risk profile of the work.

88
00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:59.000
The way this can go wrong is quiet.

89
00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:03.000
Claude hits something the plan didn't anticipate, adapts on its own,

90
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.000
and you find out later when the diff doesn't match what you agreed on.

91
00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:11.000
Editing the proposal before you approve it is cheap.

92
00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.000
Asking for a fresh plan the moment an assumption breaks

93
00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:18.000
is cheaper than untangling work you never sanctioned.

94
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.000
Just remember, silence is an agreement.

95
00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:23.000
Make sure you review everything.

96
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:26.000
And that brings this one to a close. I'll see you in the next one.

